# Working agreements

These are personal defaults for coding agents. Follow the user's current request
and the most specific applicable project instructions. Project rules may refine
these defaults, including authorization to integrate or push completed work.

## Instruction discovery

- Read the repository's `AGENTS.md` and applicable nested instructions before editing.
- For Go projects under `~/Developer/Go`, also read `~/Developer/Go/AGENTS.md`.
  This includes worktrees of those repositories outside that folder. Read the file
  explicitly; do not assume an agent discovers instructions above the Git root.
- Keep shared rules in `AGENTS.md`. Claude-specific files should import shared
  instructions rather than maintain a second copy.
- If referenced instructions or a required skill are unavailable, report that
  limitation and use available project guidance without inventing their contents.

## Local repository memory

- Use the memory directory declared in the repository's `AGENTS.md`. Read its
  `MEMORY.md` index at task start and follow only topic files relevant to the work.
  Codex, Claude, and worktrees of the same repository use that same directory.
  If no directory is declared, do not guess another repository's memory path.
- Memory is advisory context. Current user requests, applicable instructions,
  source code, and fresh verification take precedence. If memory is unavailable,
  report that briefly and continue with available guidance.
- Independently record useful, reusable lessons from user corrections or verified
  work. Include the date, lesson, evidence or source, and verification limits.
  Distinguish observations from assumptions; recheck facts that may have changed.
- Keep the index short and move detailed lessons into topic files when useful.
  Consolidate duplicates and remove stale or disproven entries. Do not require a
  memory update every task, copy existing instructions, or maintain a session log.
- Keep tasks, progress, and product decisions in the project's existing tracker
  or maintained documentation. Memory holds lessons that help future agent work.
  Never store secrets, credentials, private user data, or raw sensitive logs.
- Keep learned memory outside Git. Never stage, commit, or copy memory files into
  a repository as part of normal delivery.
- When a lesson recurs or the user confirms it, consider a small `AGENTS.md`
  improvement. Verify it against current source or tooling before promotion.
  Independently correct factual paths, commands, or outdated technical guidance;
  report the edit and verify and commit it with the normal repository workflow.
- Propose changes to permissions, Git policy, task tracking, product direction,
  or other behavioral rules for user approval before applying them. Memory never
  supplies authorization or overrides an instruction. Do not rewrite instruction
  files merely because a task ended.

## Implementation

- Prefer simple, explicit code that is easy for the next reader to follow.
- Match existing structure and conventions. Keep related behavior together.
- Stay within the requested scope. Avoid unrelated cleanup, speculative
  abstractions, and broad architectural changes.
- Preserve public APIs unless the task requires changing them.
- Prefer the standard library and existing dependencies. Add a dependency only
  when it solves a concrete problem better than the existing options.
- Preserve unrelated working-tree changes. Never reset, overwrite, or stage them
  as part of the task.

## Verification

- Add or update meaningful tests when behavior changes. Add a regression test
  for a bug fix when practical; avoid tests that only restate the implementation.
- Use repository-owned formatters and checks. Run the checks relevant to the
  change before declaring it complete.
- For UI changes, inspect the actual interaction and relevant viewport sizes.
  Passing automated tests does not establish visual approval.
- Distinguish automated tests, live integration checks, and visual inspection.
  Report failures, skipped checks, and remaining uncertainty honestly.

## Git

- Keep history linear. Never create merge commits, including through `git pull`
  or a hosting service's merge button. Use rebase for unpublished task commits
  and `git merge --ff-only` for integration.
- Do not rewrite published history or force-push without explicit authorization.
  If a fast-forward is blocked, resolve the divergence without a merge commit.
- Create focused commits after completed coding or repository-instruction work
  has been verified, unless the user asks not to. Stage only intended files.
- Ask before integrating into `main`, pushing, or publishing unless the user
  or project instructions already authorize that action. Do not ask again for
  authorization already given for the same scope.
- Preserve the repository's author and attribution conventions. Do not invent
  contributors or add a different agent's co-author trailer.

## Communication

- Be concise and concrete. Explain what changed, why, how it was checked, and
  any material limitation. Explain structural tradeoffs when they matter.
- When a decision is necessary, ask one focused question with a recommendation.
  Continue independent authorized work while waiting.
- Separate proposed work from implemented and verified behavior.
